When Does Data Value Reduce to Class Balance?A Coverage View of Per-Point Data Valuation Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2610.34801v1 [cs.LG] 26 Oct 2026 When Does Data Value Reduce to Class Balance? A Coverage View of Per-Point Data Valuation Yingjie Bai ybai0439@uni.sydney.edu.au Affiliation: School of Computer Science, The University of Sydney Weiming Zhi weiming.zhi@sydney.edu.au Affiliation: School of Computer Science, The University of Sydney Abstract Per-point data-valuation scores — Data Shapley, influence, leverage — are routinely used to select training data: keep the points with the top k k scores. We argue that top- k k by a fixed, mode-blind per-point score is the wrong selection rule precisely in the regime where data selection matters . The reason is that a point’s value is not a fixed number but depends on what else has been selected — a near-duplicate of an already-chosen point adds almost nothing, however high its score — and a per-point scalar cannot express this interaction. The theory quantifies this failure exactly: in an equal latent-mode model, fixed top- k k selection incurs a closed-form excess risk over adaptive coverage because it repeatedly spends budget on already-covered modes. The gap is largest when the budget is tight, the learner representation is redundant, and missing a mode is costly. The practical rule is simple: use balance when the labels available at selection identify the learner’s modes, and use explicit coverage when they do not. On standard classification tasks with strong pretrained features, class-balancing the same per-point scores recovers the missing coverage and matches or outperforms generic set coverage across our datasets. Without labels, label-free coverage improves accuracy by up to + 0.20 +0.20 while raw top- k k can fall below random; when labels are coarser than the modes, coverage within coarse strata performs best. We turn this distinction into a pre-specified diagnostic and validate it across both favorable and stress-test settings. The limits are part of the claim: the advantage is representation-relative. Coverage fails when the relevant modes are not expressed in the learner geometry, as with spurious subgroups, or when data quality varies independently of coverage — exactly as the theory predicts. Code: https://github.com/Benjy-byj/Data-Value-Class-Balance Reviewed on OpenReview: https://openreview.net/forum?id=AZExWUZRPd 1 Introduction Figure 1: Data value is a context function, and a fixed scalar top- k k pays an exact coverage price. Left: per-point valuation (Shapley, LOO/influence, leverage, probe-gap) compresses each training point to one number v i v_{i} , and its top- k k concentrates on a few modes of the pool (circled points), under-covering the learner’s modes: the scalar discards the set-level interaction. Middle: a point’s value is the context function g i ​ ( S ) = R ⁡ ( S ) − R ⁡ ( S ∪ { i } ) g_{i}(S)=\mathrm{R}(S)-\mathrm{R}(S\cup\{i\}) — equal to ρ ho while the point’s mode is uncovered, 0 0 once a same-mode duplicate is selected. Top right: the exact result — for any symmetric per-point score, top- k k covers only the occupancy m ⋆ ​ ( k ) m_{\star}(k) ( r ⁡ ( 1 − ( 1 − 1 / r ) k ) r(1-(1-1/r)^{k}) as N → ∞ N	o\infty ) versus adaptive greedy’s min ⁡ ( k , r ) \min(k,r) , giving 𝔼 ⁡ [ excess risk ] = ρ ​ δ N ​ ( k ) \mathbb{E}[	ext{excess risk}]=ho\,\delta_{N}(k) (a lower bound for non-coordinated scores, Definition 1 ); empirically, at k = C = 10 k=C=10 Shapley covers 6.0 6.0 and leverage 6.7 6.7 of 10 10 modes versus greedy’s 9.7 9.7 , and the synthetic binding-budget gap is 0.895 0.895 vs 0.637 0.637 ( + 0.258 +0.258 ). Bottom right: the gap matters under a binding budget, a redundant learner geometry, and a hard task, and the correction is the modes-vs-labels boundary: balance the same scores when the labels name the learner’s modes; use learner-geometry coverage when labels are missing or coarser than the modes (coarse-label CIFAR-100: coverage within coarse strata 0.419 0.419 vs. coarse-balanced prototypicality 0.240 0.240 fine accuracy); and expect no coverage handle when the representation does not express the modes (Waterbirds). Data valuation assigns each training point a scalar worth, and these scalars are widely used to select data: keep the top- k k most valuable points to train cheaply, or to curate a fine-tuning set. Data Shapley ( Ghorbani & Zou, 2019 ) , leave-one-out and influence ( Koh & Liang, 2017 ) , datamodels ( Ilyas et al., 2022 ) , and leverage scores all produce such per-point numbers. We ask whether top- k k -by-score is a good selection rule. The answer is no, precisely in the regime where data selection helps: under a binding budget, a redundant representation, and a non-interpolable task, it is provably suboptimal in a latent-mode model. Worse, “improving” the per-point score can hurt selection when the sharper score concentrates the selected set (Figure 1 previews the argument). The reason is structural. The value of a point is not a number but a function of the context of other selected points: a point that is the only representative of a concept is valuable; once a near-duplicate is selected, its marginal value collapses. A scalar summary discards exactly the information a selector needs: which points are jointly non-redundant. (Shapley averages the context function over contexts; influence evaluates it at full data.) We make this precise, prove a lower bound on the resulting selection gap, identify its source as the off-diagonal mass of the learner-geometry Gram, and verify it. Contributions. • Value is a context function (§ 2 ). A closed form g i ​ ( S ) g_{i}(S) for kernel learners; the interaction is the off-diagonal (redundant) Gram mass, and a fixed, mode-blind top- k k under-covers the modes relative to greedy coverage. • An impossibility (§ 3 ). In an equal latent-mode instance, a symmetric valuation selected top- k k has expected excess risk exactly ρ ​ δ N ​ ( k ) ho\,\delta_{N}(k) over greedy coverage, and at least this much for any non-coordinated randomized score (Definition 1 ; marginal within-mode symmetry alone is provably insufficient, Remark 1 ). The positivity range and boundary cases are characterized exactly. LOO ≡ 0 \equiv 0 , Shapley is mode-constant, and a spiked-covariance analysis gives the soft-mode extension. • The modes-vs-labels boundary (§ 4 ). Across synthetic and real ViT experiments we show that the relevant question is not whether a scalar score is accurate pointwise, but whether the groups available at selection name the learner’s modes. When modes coincide with labels, a one-line class balance of the same scores recovers the missing coverage and can outperform label-free coverage. When labels are unavailable or coarser than the modes, scalar balancing is insufficient and an explicit learner-geometry coverage or diversity correction is needed. We also delimit the claim: coverage helps only when the target modes are expressed in the learner geometry, and it can fail when the representation hides the relevant subgroups. The practical output of the paper is a one-line rule: if the labels available at selection name the learner’s modes, balance within labels; otherwise add an explicit learner-geometry coverage correction . When the modes are not expressed in the representation at all, expect neither to help. § 4.4 turns this rule into a decision procedure (Algorithm 1 ) and validates it, including its failure modes. Roadmap. § 2 sets up the one object everything else uses: a point’s value as a context function , with a closed form for kernel learners and the exact conditions under which its coverage objective is the supervised risk. § 3 proves the main result in a hard latent-mode model (§ 3.1 ), states the extensions to general randomized scores and soft modes (§ 3.2 ), and collects scope and qualifications (§ 3.3 ). § 4 organizes the experiments by role: direct validation of the theory (§ 4.1 ), the modes-vs-labels boundary on real data (§ 4.2 ), the failure modes the theory predicts (§ 4.3 ), the diagnostic (§ 4.4 ), and robustness (§ 4.5 ). 2 Value is a context function What this section establishes. Everything in the paper rests on one reframing: a training point’s value is not a scalar but a function of the already-selected set. This section defines that context function and gives it a closed form for kernel learners (Eq. 1 ). It then states precisely when the coverage objective it induces is the supervised risk, and when it is only a proxy (Proposition 1 ). Finally, it identifies the two mechanisms by which redundancy makes value context-dependent (Theorem 1 , Proposition 2 ) — the information a fixed scalar must discard. A closing paragraph fixes the terminology (modes, classes, clusters) used throughout. A learner has risk R ⁡ ( S ) = 𝔼 x ∼ D ​ ( f S ​ ( x ) − f ⋆ ​ ( x ) ) 2 \mathrm{R}(S)=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim D}(f_{S}(x)-f^{\star}(x))^{2} for a selected set S ⊆ P S\subseteq P , | P | = N |P|=N . The marginal value of point i i in context S S is g i ​ ( S ) = R ⁡ ( S ) − R ⁡ ( S ∪ { i } ) ≥ 0 g_{i}(S)=\mathrm{R}(S)-\mathrm{R}(S\cup\{i\})\geq 0 (monotone R \mathrm{R} ). Scalar valuations collapse this function to a number: leave-one-out / influence v i LOO = g i ​ ( P ∖ { i } ) v_{i}^{\mathrm{LOO}}=g_{i}(P\setminus\{i\}) ; Data Shapley v i Sh = 𝔼 π ​ g i ​ ( S < i π ) v_{i}^{\mathrm{Sh}}=\mathbb{E}_{\pi}g_{i}(S^{\pi}_{<i}) ; leverage and probe-gap are geometry- or label-derived per-point scores. The interaction is Int i = max S ⁡ g i ​ ( S ) − min S ⁡ g i ​ ( S ) \mathrm{Int}_{i}=\max_{S}g_{i}(S)-\min_{S}g_{i}(S) . Linear-algebra form. For a kernel/linear learner and point selection, the covered target is the feature second moment Σ = 𝔼 x ​ [ φ ​ φ ⊤ ] \Sigma=\m