e this regime, and the rest of the paper is precisely about them: (i) adaptive recomputation of the marginal after each pick is exactly greedy and covers min ⁡ ( k , r ) \min(k,r) ; (ii) a group-aware score (class quotas, subgroup balance) restores coverage when the groups are the modes (§ 4.2 ); and (iii) a score that already encodes mode-aware structure is, by definition, no longer mode-blind. What we claim is therefore sharp but bounded: δ N ​ ( k ) \delta_{N}(k) is the exact price of non-adaptivity — the regret of any fixed mode-blind score relative to adaptive coverage — so within this regime the remedy is structural (balance or cover), not a more accurate number. We do not claim that every conceivable scalar fails; we identify the regime in which a naive top- k k provably under-covers. 4 Experiments Findings at a glance. Scope of the empirical claims. All real-data experiments select from frozen pretrained representations with probe-family learners (ridge, an SGD-trained MLP, logistic regression, 1 1 -NN), extended by a from-scratch CNN pilot and a LoRA fine-tuning check (App. G ). The evaluation emphasizes the binding budget k ≈ C k\approx C , where the theory locates the largest gap; full k = C , 2 ​ C , 4 ​ C k=C,2C,4C sweeps are in App. G (Table 14 ). The strongest empirical claims should be read within this setting. The section is organized by the role each experiment plays in the argument: direct tests of the formal results (§ 4.1 ), the practical modes-vs-labels claim on real features (§ 4.2 ), the failure modes the theory predicts (§ 4.3 ), the decision procedure built on them (§ 4.4 ), and robustness to the estimator, encoder, modality, and hyperparameters (§ 4.5 ). One line per finding: • Direct validation (§ 4.1 ): a symmetric valuation’s top- k k agrees with the closed-form m ⋆ ​ ( k ) m_{\star}(k) within 0.007 0.007 modes, and every boundary case of the corrected theorem is verified (Table 22 ). On real pools the same formula predicts random selec